# Image to Blender

Rebuild what an image shows as a procedural 3D model in a live Blender scene.

A single image underdetermines 3D — the back of the object is a guess, scale is
an inference, materials are read from highlights. Winging it produces models
that look almost right and are unfixable because nobody knows what "right" was.
This skill converts that guesswork into checkable increments: write down what
the image shows (spec), build in locked passes, and after every pass compare a
render against the reference and record an explicit verdict. Progress is only
what a capture proves. (Methodology adapted from img2threejs.)

Work through the phases in order. Do not skip gates — each exists because the
failure it blocks is expensive to discover later.

## Phase 0 — Connect & protect

1. Call `get_scene_info`. If it fails, the Blender-side add-on isn't connected:
   read [references/setup.md](references/setup.md) and walk the user through it
   before doing anything else.
2. Note which server you're on — the exact tool names in your tool list vary
   by host and by which Blender MCP server is connected (see
   [references/setup.md](references/setup.md) for the mapping). The core loop
   only needs an `execute_blender_code`-equivalent and a way to capture a
   render; if there's no dedicated viewport-screenshot tool, rely on the
   render-to-file capture in
   [references/bpy-patterns.md](references/bpy-patterns.md) — the review loop
   uses render-to-file for formal reviews regardless.
3. `execute_blender_code` runs arbitrary Python in the user's live file. If the
   scene is not empty, ask the user to save before the first build pass, and
   confine every created or deleted object to the run's own collection.
4. Create the work dir `image2blender-runs/<subject_name>/` (with `renders/`)
   in the user's working directory. Everything the run produces — spec,
   renders — lives there, so a run can be resumed or audited later.

## Phase 1 — Intake gate

1. Read the reference image (vision) and run `scripts/probe_image.py <image>`
   — that path is relative to this SKILL.md's own directory, not your shell's
   working directory, so resolve it to an absolute path before calling it.
   Hard rejections (unknown format, tiny image) end the attempt — ask for a
   better image instead of building on sand. Surface warnings honestly.
2. Judge suitability: is there an identifiable subject? Is enough of it visible
   to infer the hidden sides? Multiple unrelated subjects → ask which one.
3. Classify: `class` (`object` | `character` | `scene`) and `complexity`
   (`simple` | `moderate` | `complex`). When in doubt between two complexity
   ratings, pick the higher one — the gates scale with it.
4. Set expectations with the user now, not after six passes: single-image
   reconstruction yields a faithful stylized approximation, not photogrammetry.
   Agree on `faithful` vs `stylized` and record it in the spec.

## Phase 2 — Spec first

Read [references/spec-format.md](references/spec-format.md), then author
`image2blender-runs/<name>/spec.json`:

1. Scan the image as a 3×3 grid, cell by cell, and list every
   identity-defining detail into `detailInventory` — the features someone who
   knows the subject would miss first. Skimping here is how builds end up
   generic; the strict gate below enforces minimums by complexity.
2. Design the component tree (one entry per future Blender object), materials
   (Principled BSDF estimates from the image), and per-pass acceptance
   criteria. Criteria must be visually checkable yes/no statements.
3. Gate: run `scripts/validate_spec.py <spec> --strict-quality` (again, resolve
   the script path against this skill's own directory). Fix and re-run until
   it passes. No build pass may run before this passes — the validator is
   deterministic and cheap; your vision time is not.

## Phase 3 — Pass-locked build

Read [references/pass-rubrics.md](references/pass-rubrics.md) and
[references/bpy-patterns.md](references/bpy-patterns.md) before the first pass.

Build order: `blockout → structure → form → materials → detail → lighting`.
One pass per iteration — never build ahead of an unreviewed pass, because an
error in proportions (blockout) invalidates every hour spent on materials.

Per pass, via `execute_blender_code`:

- Self-contained chunks (re-import, redefine helpers), small enough to stay
  under the server timeout — one component or rig per call.
- Object names = spec component ids; delete-by-name before create so re-runs
  are idempotent.
- After each chunk, verify with printed output or `get_object_info` — for
  structural criteria trust `get_scene_info` over eyeballs.

## Phase 4 — Review loop (after every pass)

1. **Capture**: render ≥2 fixed angles to
   `image2blender-runs/<name>/renders/<pass>_<angle>.png` using the pattern in
   bpy-patterns.md. Same angles every iteration so progress is comparable.
   From the materials pass on, rendered shading is mandatory.
2. **Compare**: Read the reference, then the renders. Walk this pass's
   criteria one by one; then sweep the 3×3 grid for regressions.
3. **Verdict** — exactly one, recorded in `reviewHistory` before anything else:

   | Verdict | Meaning |
   |---|---|
   | `continue` | every criterion verified in a capture → next pass |
   | `refine-spec` | spec is wrong/missing → fix spec, re-validate, rebuild pass |
   | `refine-code` | spec right, build wrong → fix only the failing criteria |
   | `request-input` | image ambiguous → ask the user, record the answer |
   | `stop` | fidelity unreachable → honest final summary |

4. **Termination**: max 3 repair attempts per pass; 2 consecutive repairs with
   no visible improvement → escalate to `request-input` with your best attempt
   shown side-by-side with the reference. Never loop silently.

## Phase 5 — Scene track

When `class` is `scene`, keep the same discipline with the scene remapping in
pass-rubrics.md: blockout places zone volumes and the camera from
`layout`, per-object mini-loops run inside form/materials batched by zone, and
lighting carries the mood. Arrangement, proportion, and palette beat per-object
detail — that's what makes a scene read.

## Phase 6 — Finish

1. Tidy: everything in the run collection, objects named per spec, transforms
   applied where sensible, origins per `pivotNote`.
2. Final captures: the three review angles plus one framing that matches the
   reference composition.
3. Report to the user: what matches the reference, what is deliberately
   stylized, what the image didn't reveal (and was therefore invented). Honest
   limitations beat implied perfection — the user will see them anyway.
4. Offer exports: save as `.blend` or export glTF/FBX/OBJ — only with the
   user's go-ahead, and never overwrite their file without asking.

## Asset policy

Geometry is modeled procedurally (primitives, modifiers, curves, geometry
nodes) — that is the point of the skill: editable, animation-ready results.
Poly Haven textures/HDRIs are welcome where `textureStrategy: polyhaven` says
so (bundled server only). Hyper3D/Sketchfab model generation or import is
opt-in: only when the user explicitly prefers speed over editability, and
record it as `strategy: asset` in the spec.

